CC   -!- FUNCTION: Binds to host cell surface receptors and mediates fusion
CC       between viral and cellular membranes. Efficient virus attachment to
CC       cell is, at least in part, mediated by host HAVCR1 in a cell-type
CC       specific manner. In addition, host NCAM1 can also be used as entry
CC       receptor. Interaction with host HSPA5 plays an important role in the
CC       early stages of infection as well. Envelope protein is synthesized in
CC       the endoplasmic reticulum and forms a heterodimer with protein prM. The
CC       heterodimer plays a role in virion budding in the ER, and the newly
CC       formed immature particle is covered with 60 spikes composed of
CC       heterodimers between precursor prM and envelope protein E. The virion
CC       is transported to the Golgi apparatus where the low pH causes the
CC       dissociation of PrM-E heterodimers and formation of E homodimers. PrM-E
CC       cleavage is inefficient, many virions are only partially matured and
CC       immature prM-E proteins could play a role in immune evasion.
CC       {ECO:0000256|ARBA:ARBA00053968}.
CC   -!- FUNCTION: Functions as a signal peptide for NS4B and is required for
CC       the interferon antagonism activity of the latter.
CC       {ECO:0000256|ARBA:ARBA00003504}.
CC   -!- FUNCTION: Induces the formation of ER-derived membrane vesicles where
CC       the viral replication takes place. Also plays a role in the inhibition
CC       of host RLR-induced interferon-beta production at TANK-binding kinase
CC       1/TBK1 level. Cooperatively with NS4A suppresses the Akt-mTOR pathway
CC       and leads to cellular dysregulation. {ECO:0000256|ARBA:ARBA00060220}.
CC   -!- FUNCTION: Inhibits RNA silencing by interfering with host Dicer.
CC       {ECO:0000256|ARBA:ARBA00035616}.
CC   -!- FUNCTION: May play a role in virus budding. Exerts cytotoxic effects by
CC       activating a mitochondrial apoptotic pathway through M ectodomain. May
CC       display a viroporin activity. {ECO:0000256|ARBA:ARBA00035609}.
CC   -!- FUNCTION: Plays a role in host immune defense modulation and protection
CC       of envelope protein E during virion synthesis. PrM-E cleavage is
CC       inefficient, many virions are only partially matured and immature prM-E
CC       proteins could play a role in immune evasion. Contributes to fetal
CC       microcephaly in humans. Acts as a chaperone for envelope protein E
CC       during intracellular virion assembly by masking and inactivating
CC       envelope protein E fusion peptide. prM is the only viral peptide
CC       matured by host furin in the trans-Golgi network probably to avoid
CC       catastrophic activation of the viral fusion activity in acidic Golgi
CC       compartment prior to virion release. {ECO:0000256|ARBA:ARBA00053204}.
CC   -!- FUNCTION: Plays a role in the inhibition of host RLR-induced
CC       interferon-beta activation by targeting TANK-binding kinase 1/TBK1. In
CC       addition, recruits the host deubiquitinase USP8 to cleave 'Lys-11'-
CC       linked polyubiquitin chains from caspase-1/CASP1 thus inhibiting its
CC       proteasomal degradation. In turn, stabilized CASP1 promotes cleavage of
CC       cGAS, which inhibits its ability to recognize mitochondrial DNA release
CC       and initiate type I interferon signaling.
CC       {ECO:0000256|ARBA:ARBA00053213}.
CC   -!- FUNCTION: Plays a role in virus budding by binding to the cell membrane
CC       and gathering the viral RNA into a nucleocapsid that forms the core of
CC       the mature virus particle. During virus entry, may induce genome
CC       penetration into the host cytoplasm after hemifusion induced by the
CC       surface proteins. Can migrate to the cell nucleus where it modulates
CC       host functions. Inhibits the integrated stress response (ISR) in the
CC       infected cell. {ECO:0000256|ARBA:ARBA00053869}.
CC   -!- FUNCTION: Prevents premature fusion activity of envelope proteins in
CC       trans-Golgi by binding to envelope protein E at pH 6.0. After virion
CC       release in extracellular space, gets dissociated from E dimers.
CC       {ECO:0000256|ARBA:ARBA00054739}.
CC   -!- FUNCTION: Required cofactor for the serine protease function of NS3.
CC       {ECO:0000256|ARBA:ARBA00056013}.
